  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow amplitude upper level ridging across the western US will \nmaintain unseasonably warm and dry conditions across the area \nthrough Friday beneath a west-northwest flow aloft. Afternoon \ntemperatures will run slightly lower than those of Monday but still \naround 15 degrees above early October climatological averages. \nAfternoon mixing and a moderate surface pressure gradient across the \nRockies will support some breeziness this afternoon, mainly across \nthe plains, with overall lighter winds Wed and Thu as surface high \npressure slides east across the Canadian Prairies. \n\nAn upper level trough digging southeast through BC Friday will bring \na significant weather pattern change for the weekend with windy \nconditions developing Friday as southwesterly mid-level flow \nincreases ahead of the approaching trough. Warm, windy and dry \nconditions Friday will bring elevated fire weather concerns ahead of \na cold front Friday night that brings in much cooler and wetter \nconditions for the weekend. Model guidance is in reasonable \nagreement to dig the trough southward into the NW US Saturday with \ntypical differences and spread still at this time range among \nmodels/ensemble members in the eventual evolution of the trough \nthrough the rest of the weekend into early next week.  Hoenisch\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWith warm and dry conditions expected to continue through the rest \nof the week, there is a concern for elevated fire weather conditions \n(see FIRE WEATHER for more information).\n\n\nWintry Conditions This Weekend:\n\nConfidence continues to increase in the potential for cold \ntemperatures and widespread snow along the mountains this weekend \nwith the potential for some lower elevation snow Sunday morning. \nTemperatures on Saturday will be 25-30 degrees cooler compared to \nFriday and continue to drop another 15-20 degrees by Sunday.\n\nThere are hints in the models that some of the heavier mountain snow \ncould arrive Saturday evening instead of early Sunday morning. This \nhas the potential to impact more of the Continental Divide and the \nisland ranges of Central Montana with some additional snow possible \ncompared to the previous storm totals. The main impacts would be \nalong mountain passes, particularly Logan and Marias Pass initially \nfollowed by Rogers Pass over the next few hours. Those who plan on \nrecreating or traveling this weekend should have a plan for cold \ntemperatures and wet snow. \n\nAcross the northern plains there is a potential for snow levels to \nfluctuate right around ground level through Sunday afternoon which \ncould produce a rain/snow mix at times. Additional snowfall \naccumulations are expected to be on the lighter side by this point \nin the event but a larger concern might be if there is any rain/snow \nmix plus daytime melting on Sunday followed by subfreezing \ntemperatures overnight into Monday morning which could make roads \nand sidewalks slick across the area.\n\nGiven the level of uncertainty still present in the models it is \nhard to pin down exactly how much snow will fall during this event \nbut there is at least a 50% probability of locations below 5000 feet \nseeing at least one inch of snow total. -thor",
